answer:
According to Article 10.07 Annual Academic Workload of the Collective Agreement 2018-2021 (page 20), the President must ensure that the annual academic workload is determined, distributed, and scheduled as equitably as possible. The member's assigned annual academic workload must take into account various factors, including:

(i) the needs of the member's program, including student enrolments and the necessity of offering required courses;
(ii) the number of hours of preparation, grading and administration per course;
(iii) whether the member will be responsible for giving unscheduled courses such as directed readings;
(iv) the number of hours of academic counselling and consulting per course;
(v) the level (introductory, upper year, graduate, etc.) of each course;
(vi) the assignment of markers or teaching assistants;
(vii) additional hours of preparation for a new course, or for a course substantially revised;
(viii) the relationship of teaching duties to the member's area of expertise;
(ix) the amount of field, research, thesis, and other academic supervision of undergraduate and graduate students;
(x) the method of delivery of each course.

Additionally, Article 10.07(c)(i) states that teaching workloads shall be assigned by the President upon consideration of the recommendation of the Department Chair pursuant to Article 9.06(g).

The collective agreement also provides specific standards for full-time faculty workload in Article 10.07(c)(iv), which states that it shall be a standard that full-time faculty shall have a normal teaching load of 15 credits per academic year, including a minimum of 12 credits taught live.

For Sessional Members, the collective agreement provides specific provisions regarding their workload in Article 4.25, including limitations on the number of credits they can teach and requirements for cancellation of contracts.

